From 03e88fe86eeb3b8002cd92ae437147e32171eeb7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Martin Ellis <malard@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 22:27:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: Stop the savestate capture test racing its
 worker thread (#29745)

TestSavestateCapture.FailedExplicitSerializationReleasesStorageWithoutCapturingMetadata
took the released snapshot back with TryAcquire(), which gives up if it cannot
have the worker's mutex at once. The Release() just before it notifies the worker
thread, which wakes and takes that mutex to re-test its wait condition. When the
two met, TryAcquire() returned null and the test failed at its null assert, with
nothing wrong in the code under test.

Acquire() waits for the mutex, as TestSavestateWorker.ReturnsStorageAfterFailure
already does to show that storage came back. It never returns null, so the assert
goes with it.

Unchanged, the test failed 355 times in 20000 repeats on Windows x64. With this
change the suite's two tests passed 100000 repeats.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 685dc56fdcaa97302e733abcae144350fa53cd1f)
---
 xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/playback/test/TestSavestateCapture.cpp | 3 +--
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/playback/test/TestSavestateCapture.cpp b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/playback/test/TestSavestateCapture.cpp
index e738b11..0629a1b 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/playback/test/TestSavestateCapture.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/playback/test/TestSavestateCapture.cpp
@@ -102,8 +102,7 @@ TEST(TestSavestateCapture, FailedExplicitSerializationReleasesStorageWithoutCapt
   EXPECT_EQ(metadataCaptures, 1u);
   if (snapshot)
     worker.Release(snapshot);
-  snapshot = worker.TryAcquire();
-  ASSERT_NE(snapshot, nullptr);
+  snapshot = worker.Acquire();
   EXPECT_EQ(snapshot->memory.get(), address);
   worker.Release(snapshot);
 }
